 #### Noise Coordinate Systems
 - **Definition:** Tying noise functions to a coordinate system for geometric primitives, requiring smooth and quick transformations in and out of the local space [S1, unofficial].
-- **Steps:** For a sphere, use object space as the noise coordinate space, with transformations calculated as simple matrix multiplications. For a curve or spline, use a coordinate system deforming with the spline, with range $[0,1]$ along the spline and $[-1,1]$ along the two radius axes, using Tangent (T), Normal (N), and Cross(N, T) as basis vectors. For a surface patch, range is $[0,1]$ along each surface dimension and $[-1,1]$ along the normal, using dP/du, dP/dv, and Normal (N) as basis vectors [S1, unofficial].
+- **Steps:** For a sphere, use object space as the noise coordinate space, with transformations calculated as simple matrix multiplications [S1, unofficial]. For a curve or spline, use a coordinate system deforming with the spline, with range $[0,1]$ along the spline and $[-1,1]$ along the two radius axes, using Tangent (T), Normal (N), and Cross(N, T) as basis vectors [S1, unofficial]. For a surface patch, range is $[0,1]$ along each surface dimension and $[-1,1]$ along the normal, using dP/du, dP/dv, and Normal (N) as basis vectors [S1, unofficial].
 - **Pitfall:** Transformations for curves and surfaces are costly compared to spheres, especially for surfaces composed of arbitrary parametric primitives requiring search [S1, unofficial].
 
 #### Noise-based 2D Fire
-- **Steps:** Multiply Voronoi noise and Perlin noise textures. Interpolate the product of the noises with a standard UV texture to create a modified UV coordinate. Sample a single Gaussian blob using the modified UV to create fire-like variations. Apply a color gradient to give an orange color to the result [S2, unofficial].
